
\subsection{Performance Profiler}
\label{subsec:profiler}

Given a linear layer with weight $W_{INT4} \in \mathbb{N}^{N \times K}$ and an input activation $A_{FP16} \in \mathbb{R}^{M \times K}$, we design several  quantization strategies for computation, as depicted in Figure \ref{fig:compt_strategy}.
\begin{itemize}
\item $W_c4A_c16$ (W4A16 GEMV/GEMM):
Since the weights in storage is 4-bit, $W_c4A_c16$ is the original strategy for computation.
We can directly use $W_{INT4}$ in the W4A16 GEMV/GEMM kernel to calculate the result.
The W4A16 kernel fuse dequantization and matrix multiplication (Matmul) into one kernel to reduce memory access overhead~\cite{AWQ, GPTQ, marlin, ladder}.
The GEMV kernel only uses CUDA Core, while the GEMM kernel uses Tensor Core for matrix multiplication~\cite{}.
Above works relied on an empirical threshold to switch between GEMV and GEMM kernels. 
For instance, when M is less than 8, GEMV is used, otherwise GEMM is used.

We demonstrate that giving an empirical threshold is inefficient.
For a linear layer with a weight shape of (1024, 4096), GEMV outperforms GEMM when M < 19.
For a shape of (4096, 14336), GEMM becomes faster when M > 5.
Therefore, we separate GEMV and GEMM, and employ the profiler to determine the threshold for each linear layer.
The $W_c4A_c16$ quantization strategy effectively reduces weight memory access and is suitable for the memory-bound prefill phase when M is small.

\item $W_c8A_c8$ (W8A8 GEMM): 
In order to use W8A8 GEMM, we need to convert the precision of weights by dequantize $W_{INT4}$ back to INT8 format.
The $W_c8A_c8$ quantization strategy requires to perform online quantization to acitvations.
This strategy leverages the hardware integer computation units (e.g., INT8 Tensor Core).
Thus, it can obtain acceleration in the compute-bound prefill phase.
Since $W_c8A_c8$ is used for larger M, it is unnecessary to consider W8A8 GEMV.
The same applies to $W_c16A_c16$.

\item $W_c16A_c16$ (FP16 GEMM): We dequantize $W_{INT4}$ back to 16-bit, and execute the FP16 GEMM kernel with the full precision activation $A_{FP16}$.
Compared to the $W_c4A_c16$, this strategy executes dequantization and Matmul kernels separately.
The $W_c16A_c16$ strategy has a shallower slope than $W_c14A_c16$, as shown in Figure~\ref{eq:latency}(c).
Therefore, by Theorem~\ref{theorem}, this strategy is better than $W_c4A_c16$ when M is large enough.
% \item Path 4(W4A8):
\end{itemize}

In order to select a better quantization strategy $W_cxA_cy$ at runtime, we design an offline performance profiler. 
% The profiler aims to build a lookup table of the optimal computation path based on workload characteristics for a given hardware platform and linear layer.
We design the profiler based on following two observations.

\textbf{Observation 1: The input dimension $M$ is the key variable determining the workload characteristics.}
In a linear layer, the dimensions K and N are fixed. 
Therefore, the workload characteristic is determined by the first dimension M of the input tensor, which is the product of batch size and sequence length.

In the decoding phase, the length of each input sequence is 1. Therefore, M is equal to the batch size.
In the prefill phase, the sequence length is determined by user input and is dynamically  changing.
Thus, we can simplify the optimal path selection problem to a function of the single variable M:
\begin{equation}
P(M) = argmin_{Path \in {0,1,2,3}} T_{Path}(M)
\end{equation}
where $T_{Path}(M)$ denotes the end-to-end execution latency for a given path at a specific M.

\textbf{Observation 2: The shapes of linear layers in LLMs are few.}
Modern LLMs typically employ a homogeneous, Transformer-based architecture. 
This means that the shapes of linear layers (e.g., Q, K, V, O projections, and the two layers in the Feed-Forward Network) are determined by only a few configuration parameters (e.g., hidden dimension, number of attention heads, FFN intermediate expansion factor). 
For example, Llama3 8B have only four weight shapes in all Transformer layers, and Llama3 70B is the same.

This observation is critical because it means we only need to build and store the M-based lookup tables for a limited set of weight shapes to cover the entire model. 
This design keeps the overhead of offline profiling and the runtime memory overhead of the lookup tables minimal, ensuring the practicality and efficiency of our framework.


As shown in Figure \ref{fig:profiler}, based on the above observations, the workflow of profiler is as follows:

\textbf{1) Shape Clustering and Initialization.}
Firstly, analyze all linear layers in the target model architecture and cluster them based on the shape (N, K) of their weight tensors.
For each unique shape, initialize an empty lookup table $L_{(N, K)}$.

\textbf{2) Representative Workload Sampling.}
For each unique shape, sample a representative range of M, (e.g., $M \in [1, 2, 4, 8, ..., M_{max}]$), where $M_{max}$ corresponds to the maximum expected product of batch size and sequence length during the prefill phase. 

\textbf{3) Benchmarking and Performance Profiling.}
For each sampled (M, K, N) triplet, the profiler performs benchmarking to obtain the average latencies:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Ensure that no other programs are running on the hardware during testing.
    \item Prepare a random 4-bit weight tensor $W_{INT4}  \in \mathbb{N}^{N \times K}$ quantized by TSQ method and a random activation tensor $A_{FP16} \in \mathbb{R}^{M \times K}$.
    \item Execute every computation paths separately.
    \item Measure the average latency for each path to obtain stable values $T_{path}$.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{4) Lookup Table Construction.}
For each sampled $M_i$, compare the measured latencies of all paths and record the optimal path $P(M_i)$ into the shape's lookup table $L_{shape}$.
After the construction is completed, traverse $L_{shape}$ and merge adjacent identical options to obtain an interval lookup table.
Finally, save the lookup table for runtime quantization strategy decision.



